{
  "teamId": 135,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 San Diego Padres arrived with World Series ambitions, a superstar in Fernando Tatis Jr., and a roster built to finally break San Diego's playoff drought into a sustained run. The early months delivered on the promise — a sizzling April went 15-12, and May erupted at 19-9, fueled by grand slams, Tatis moonshots, and a lineup that looked capable of carrying the franchise to October glory. Jurickson Profar's clutch doubles, Eric Hosmer delivering in the clutch against the Dodgers, and Victor Caratini's grand slam in May painted a picture of a team that could manufacture runs from anywhere in the order.\n\nJune kept the engine running at 15-12, with Tatis blasting his 19th homer as part of another grand slam, Ha-Seong Kim contributing key blasts, and Trent Grisham capping the month with a walk-off grand slam on June 30th. But cracks were forming in the bullpen — Mark Melancon, supposed to be the reliable closer, was getting torched at the worst moments, surrendering crushing home runs that erased hard-fought leads. The team still looked like a contender through the All-Star break, sitting in postseason position and drawing national attention as one of the NL's most dangerous clubs.\n\nJuly turned the tide. The 13-14 record masked a deeper rot setting in — Melancon continued to blow saves, Emilio Pagán surrendered a brutal two-run shot to Juan Soto in mid-July, and the bullpen that was supposed to be a strength became a liability. August brought more of the same at 11-15, with late-game meltdowns piling up even as offensive heroes like Jake Cronenworth and Tatis himself kept trying to will the team forward. The gap between the Padres' ceiling and their floor had never felt wider, and the playoff window was quietly sliding shut.\n\nSeptember delivered the final, devastating verdict: a 7-19 collapse that turned a team with October dreams into a cautionary tale about bullpen construction and late-season staying power. Emilio Pagán's worst moment came on September 18th, a -0.620 WPA catastrophe when Tyler O'Neill's two-run homer crushed what could have been a momentum-saving win against St. Louis. The Padres finished 79-83, four games below .500, scoring more runs than they allowed yet finding a way to lose — a damning indictment of a bullpen that squandered the season one blown lead at a time.",
